1
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:10,460
in any modern developed economy, based largely on the fact that we perversely insist on living

2
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:18,440
beyond age 45, and therefore make provisions for the future, which made no sense when you

3
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:26,660
expected to die at age 45, and the only sensible provision was to have enough sons.

4
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:33,390
That nobody has ever looked upon as capital formation, though it is, believe me.

5
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:41,940
So you will have a continuing pressure on capital formation and continuing high costs

6
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:44,630
of capital.

7
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:50,040
And yet less capital will have less and less power.

8
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:56,840
The power will increasingly lie in the ability to organize the knowledge resources and to

9
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:58,400
market.

10
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:07,070
And this, I think, is a new structure, which I do not pretend to understand.

11
00:01:07,070 --> 00:01:13,300
But when I look at my friends in business, I don't think they're organized for it yet.

12
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:19,160
And we are quite rightly putting our chief financial officer into a key position, and

13
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,640
you better stay there.

14
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:28,580
And when you talk of top management, increasingly the chief financial officer is not the subordinate

15
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:33,320
of the chief executive, or if he is, the company is mismanaged.

16
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:39,930
He has very largely a power centre of his own and has his own relationship to the board

17
00:01:39,930 --> 00:01:42,350
with our new audit requirements and so on.

18
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:53,400
But I think we will have to do very much the same in respect to the mobilisation of the knowledge of the company.

19
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,160
Marketing is largely knowledge and not investment.

20
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:03,490
The knowledge resources, I think the organisation of tomorrow will be far more of a team

21
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:10,990
even than it is today, precisely because one will not be able to control that affiliate in Germany

22
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:16,190
through capital, or that subcontractor in Nigeria through capital,

23
00:02:16,590 --> 00:02:21,110
but only through design and management and marketing.

24
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:30,160
And I think this is a structural change which I can kind of divine, but cannot put my hands on.

25
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:36,060
And if you ask me how do we do it, I'm willing to say retain me for five years

26
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:38,920
and we'll make enough mistakes to know what not to do.

27
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,200
But I'm not yet willing to say that I or anybody else knows how to do it.

28
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:50,440
And I think, therefore, that the international economy all will force us into rethinking again

29
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,700
how one structures top management.

30
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:59,280
We talked yesterday about other demands on top management out of the political arena.

31
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:16,100
I think we are at the beginning of 10 years of very hard work on rethinking the task, the qualifications, the structure of and the preparations for top management.

32
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:45,520
A job which we thought we had done around 1960, I think we are going to have to start all over again because the environment has changed so drastically, both domestically and worldwide, from the one which we assumed when we drew up the last blueprint and the buildings we then constructed will, I think, become well about as unlivable as the hotel in which I'm staying now,

33
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:50,860
which was built in 1920 on the assumption that there are abundant servants.

34
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:56,400
And I think we will have to redesign our new assumptions.

35
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:58,540
Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen.

36
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:10,180
Thank you very much, Peter. This doesn't work, does it?

37
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:12,220
You have a strong voice.

38
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:19,000
For the benefit of the audience, when I mentioned earlier that there was a big stack of questions here,

39
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:20,060
there were yesterday's questions.

40
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:34,760
So after hearing what you just heard, you may, as I have, develop some questions which you may address to Peter Drucker or Irving Friedman later.

41
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:50,320
My next task is to introduce Irving Friedman, who has a rather long and interesting and important career in some of the most important international monetary organizations in the world.

42
00:04:50,320 --> 00:05:00,720
For 18 years, he was in the International Monetary Fund and graduated from there as the head of the international economic staff

43
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:08,320
and moved, I guess, across the street, or maybe in 1964 it was in the same building, to another wing, to the World Bank,

44
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:13,600
where he served for 10 years from 1964 to 1974

45
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:20,560
and again was the head of the economic research staff in the World Bank.

46
00:05:21,930 --> 00:05:24,090
In 1974, he became a real banker

47
00:05:24,090 --> 00:05:29,750
and went to a bank which had a significant amount of exposure in developing countries,

48
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:32,770
at least that was a rumor, called the Citicorp,

49
00:05:33,270 --> 00:05:37,350
and recently moved to the first Boston Corporation

50
00:05:37,350 --> 00:05:40,930
where he is currently Senior International Advisor, Irving Friedman.
